  "text": "Question ten. What is class imbalance, and how do you handle it? When one class dominates, accuracy stops meaning anything: calling every transaction legitimate scores ninety-nine percent, and catches nothing. Judge by precision, recall or pee-ar ay-you-see. Then weight the rare class in the loss, oversample it with smote's synthetic rows or undersample the majority, move the threshold, or at the extreme, treat it as anomaly detection. The follow-up is what reweighting does. With balanced class weights, recall at point five jumps from twenty-four percent to eighty-four. But the model now calls the average transaction twenty-two percent likely to be fraud, when one percent is, and its pee-ar ay-you-see falls from point four six to point three two. So if anything downstream reads those as probabilities, recalibrate. That won't win back the ranking, so try lowering the plain model's threshold first. And resample only the training set: the test set keeps production's one percent.",
